{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WQ3ovVDEmUFBYXp2JUGznE","type":"tool_result","content":"dict_keys(['E10', 'E11', 'E12'])\nE11 never ['E11.1', 'E11.2', 'E11.3', 'E11.4']\nE12 never []\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Retention set to cover the investigation window and legal needs (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk logs).\n- Integrity checks on audit records (hash chains, signed batches, sequence numbers).\n- Durable local buffering with delivery receipts, backlog limits, and alerts on gaps.\n- High-impact actions fail closed when audit capture is down.\n- Documented export, restore, redaction, and access rules.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Audit writes are fire-and-forget; failures are logged and the action continues.\n- Default backend retention (for example 7 or 30 days) with no longer archive.\n- No alert when log delivery stops or sequence numbers skip.\n- Exported logs hold unredacted sensitive data with broad read access.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- audit logging client and buffering code\n- log backend and bucket lifecycle settings\n- monitoring and alert rules for log pipelines\n- data retention and redaction policy docs\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E11 recipe: restore an old trace, check integrity, interrupt delivery and inject a missing event, and confirm the gap is caught and high-impact actions stop.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E12 · G3 · C7/C8 — Fleet incident response\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations, Governance, Business owner.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRun a drill where a shared tool or agent type is compromised. Find the affected instances and tenants. Quarantine the dependency, revoke the access involved, stop affected work, and alert the named responders. Check that alerts about abuse across agents, and about budgets, reach the team that can act. Name who decides whether to report outside the organization and share information.\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nMark a shared test tool or version as compromised. Query which instances and tenants it affects, quarantine it, revoke the access involved, and trigger response alerts. Affected work must stop within the set targets. Responders must get identities and traces they can act on. Record who decided on outside reporting and information sharing.\n\n**Evidence to keep:** Fleet impact query, quarantine and revocation records, alert delivery and response times.\n\n### Checks for likely_pass\n\nChecks for likely_pass:\n\n- (none) Code can't show this item. Don't rate it likely_pass; use needs_evidence, gap, or na_candidate.\n\n### Not covered here\n\nFile these problems under their owning item, not this one, unless this item's own requirement is also broken:\n\n- Kill switch for a single agent not drilled → **R11**\n- No owner named on each side of a shared control → **E01**\n- Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning → **E02**\n- Budget and rate limits not enforced outside the model → **B07**\n\n### Heuristics\n\n**Provable from code:** no\n\n**Look for:**\n- An inventory or query that maps tool and dependency versions to instances and tenants.\n- Quarantine controls for a shared tool or agent type (gateway deny rule, registry block, feature flag).\n- Alert routes for cross-agent abuse and budget alerts to a named responder team.\n- An incident runbook that names who decides on outside reporting and information sharing.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- No way to find which deployments or tenants use a given tool version.\n- Quarantine requires a code change and full redeploy.\n- Alerts go to an unowned channel or a single person.\n- No named decision-maker for outside reporting.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- incident runbooks and on-call config\n- alert routing config (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, Alertmanager)\n- gateway and tool registry block rules\n- SBOM or dependency inventory systems\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Almost all of this item is shown by the E12 drill: mark a shared tool compromised, query the impact, quarantine, revoke, alert, and measure stop times. Code only shows that the controls exist.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n# hr-helpdesk-agent\n\nAn HR helpdesk assistant for Acme employees. It answers policy questions from the HR handbook,\nshows the signed-in employee their own leave balance, files leave requests (the manager approves\nthem in HRIS), and hands conversations to an HR advisor.\n\n## Components\n\n| Component | Code | Identity |\n|---|---|---|\n| Agent API and loop | `src/main.py`, `src/agent.py` | SA `hr-helpdesk-agent`, SPIFFE SVID |\n| Tool gateway | `src/gateway.py` | SA `hr-tool-gateway` |\n| HRIS MCP server | owned by hris-platform | `spiffe://acme.example/ns/hris/sa/hris-mcp` |\n| Helpdesk MCP server | owned by it-service-desk | `spiffe://acme.example/ns/helpdesk/sa/helpdesk-mcp` |\n| Audit, approvals, quotas, stop flags, handbook index | Postgres (`migrations/001_init.sql`) | per-component DB roles |\n\n## How a request flows\n\n1. The HR portal sends the employee's SSO token. `main.py` verifies issuer, audience, expiry and\n   organisation; the employee id and tenant come only from that token.\n2. The loop (`agent.py`) checks the stop flags and the run budget before every model call, and\n   records usage against per-user, tenant and fleet quotas.\n3. Tool calls are looked up by exact name in `config/tools.yaml`. Arguments are schema-checked.\n   `file_leave_request` and `escalate_to_human` pause the run and show the employee exactly what\n   will be sent; the approval is bound to the run instance, operation, target and argument hash,\n   expires after 10 minutes, and is consumed once.\n4. MCP calls go to the gateway with a 300-second token for that one tool scope, minted by STS\n   from the agent's workload identity and the employee's token. The gateway verifies the token\n   and its certificate binding, checks scope, stop flags, rate limits and argument schema, swaps\n   the token for one addressed to the MCP server, and validates the response schema.\n5. Tool output is size-, schema- and content-checked and redacted before the model sees it.\n   Handbook passages are filtered at query time to documents the employee may see.\n6. Every step writes an `audit.events` row with the six identity fields and trace context.\n   If the audit write fails, the run stops.\n\nEscalations are delivered even during gateway maintenance windows.\n\n## Stopping the agent\n\nOperators with the `hr_agent_operator` DB role insert into `control.stop_flags`:\n\n```sql\nINSERT INTO control.stop_flags (scope, value, reason) VALUES ('fleet', NULL, 'incident 2291');\n-- or scope 'agent_type' / 'agent_instance' / 'subject' / 'agent_principal'\n```\n\nRunning agents see the flag within 2 seconds and cancel in-flight work; the gateway refuses\ncalls immediately. Target stop time: 10 seconds. The agent's DB role can only read this table.\nLast drill: see `docs/drills/` in the ops repo.\n\n## Audit retention\n\nAudit records are kept for six months (Acme records policy; employment-related AI use in the EU).\nPartitions are created and dropped nightly by `k8s/audit-maintenance.yaml`. Access is limited to\n`hr_audit_reader`.\n\n## Releases\n\nMerges to `main` build, sign and attest the image, compute the agent-type-id over every file in\n`src/`, `prompts/`, `config/`, plus `requirements.txt`, `Dockerfile`, the image digest and the model\nrecord, and stamp `release/manifest.yaml`. Pods refuse to start if the type id they compute\ndiffers from the one they were deployed with. Kyverno admits only signed images built by this\nworkflow from `main`.\n*                         @acme/people-platform\n/prompts/                 @acme/people-platform @acme/hr-policy-reviewers\n/config/                  @acme/people-platform @acme/security-engineering\n/src/gateway.py           @acme/people-platform @acme/security-engineering\n/src/approvals.py         @acme/people-platform @acme/security-engineering\n/k8s/                     @acme/people-platform @acme/platform-security\n/migrations/              @acme/people-platform @acme/data-platform\n/release/                 @acme/people-platform @acme/security-engineering\n/.github/                 @acme/platform-security\n/CODEOWNERS               @acme/platform-security","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3e66afe2-1846-4ff8-b308-c4a55d4d1400","uuid":"42001eff-ec96-4183-abc8-ae9a4ff89a47","timestamp":"2026-11-21T12:15:06.141Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"dict_keys(['E10', 'E11', 'E12'])\nE11 never ['E11.1', 'E11.2', 'E11.3', 'E11.4']\nE12 never []\n\n**Provable from code:** partly\n\n**Look for:**\n- Retention set to cover the investigation window and legal needs (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk logs).\n- Integrity checks on audit records (hash chains, signed batches, sequence numbers).\n- Durable local buffering with delivery receipts, backlog limits, and alerts on gaps.\n- High-impact actions fail closed when audit capture is down.\n- Documented export, restore, redaction, and access rules.\n\n**Red flags:**\n- Audit writes are fire-and-forget; failures are logged and the action continues.\n- Default backend retention (for example 7 or 30 days) with no longer archive.\n- No alert when log delivery stops or sequence numbers skip.\n- Exported logs hold unredacted sensitive data with broad read access.\n\n**Where to look:**\n- audit logging client and buffering code\n- log backend and bucket lifecycle settings\n- monitoring and alert rules for log pipelines\n- data retention and redaction policy docs\n\n**Needs runtime evidence:** Needs the E11 recipe: restore an old trace, check integrity, interrupt delivery and inject a missing event, and confirm the gap is caught and high-impact actions stop.\n\n**N/A when:** never\n\n## E12 · G3 · C7/C8 — Fleet incident response\n\n**Lead:** Security. **Shares:** Operations, Governance, Business owner.\n\n### Requirement\n\nRun a drill where a sh